# Multi-repository workflow rules

## When this applies

Use when the product spans **more than one repository**, or a tree with clear frontend/backend folders. Pure library repos (e.g. NuGet `Cross.*` without `client/` / `server/`) usually skip these path conventions unless those folders exist.

## Typical layout

- Frontend — Angular (or similar) under `client/`, `client/apps/` + `client/libs/`, or `apps/` + `libs/`
- Backend — .NET under `server/` (or a dedicated API repository)

Example product split (not required for every repo): **gurusolutions.co** (`client/`) + **gurusolutions.co.api** (`server/`).

## Working with paths

- Frontend work — paths relative to `client/` (or `client/apps/` / `client/libs/` / `apps/` / `libs/`)
- Backend work — paths relative to `server/` when that tree exists
- Use full paths when ambiguous

## API consistency

- When changing the API on the backend, update corresponding interfaces and services on the frontend
- Prefer endpoint constants (`API_ENDPOINTS` or host equivalent), not raw URL string literals
- Follow API versioning in the path (`api/v{version:apiVersion}`) when the host uses it

## Shared resources

- Translations — are in `**/assets/i18n/`
- Shared types and contracts must stay consistent between frontend and backend
